      "id": "insulin-team-and-scale",
      "title": "Insulin required four researchers",
      "question": "Did Banting work alone?",
      "body": "**Documented fact.** Frederick Banting proposed experiments. John Macleod supplied facilities. He supplied dogs too. Charles Best assisted experiments. James Collip purified extracts. Leonard Thompson received one. The first injection disappointed. Collip’s improved extract worked. That happened during January 1922. Banting, Best, and Collip patented production. Each assigned rights cheaply. Toronto received those rights. The university licensed manufacturers. Eli Lilly helped scale production. The 1923 Nobel recognized Banting. It also recognized Macleod. Both shared prize money. Their choices acknowledged colleagues. **Scholarly interpretation.** Insulin emerged through collaboration. Laboratory access mattered greatly. Purification made treatment practical. Manufacturing spread its benefits. **Benefits.** Insulin transformed fatal diabetes. Licensing enabled wider supply. **Conflicts and harms.** Credit disputes damaged relationships. Early supply stayed scarce. Production remained technically difficult. **Counterevidence and limits.** Banting’s idea mattered greatly. Macleod did not originate it. Yet solo-inventor stories fail. Modern insulin prices differ. They cannot be blamed solely. The 1923 licensing system changed. Later analogues added patents.",
