      "title": "Penicillin needed development",
      "question": "Was Fleming sufficient?",
      "body": "**Documented fact.** Alexander Fleming observed inhibition. That happened during 1928. His mold produced penicillin. He published during 1929. Stable production remained elusive. Oxford researchers returned later. Howard Florey led them. Ernst Chain investigated chemistry. Norman Heatley built apparatus. Their team tested mice. Human treatment followed during 1941. Supplies initially ran short. Florey and Heatley sought Americans. Federal laboratories assisted fermentation. Pharmaceutical companies increased yields. War contracts accelerated scale. Fleming, Chain, and Florey shared Nobel recognition. **Scholarly interpretation.** Observation began the pathway. Translation required organized development. Fermentation converted promise into medicine. **Benefits.** Penicillin treated lethal infections. Wartime production built capacity. **Conflicts and harms.** Public storytelling favored Fleming. Heatley received delayed recognition. Antibiotic success encouraged overuse. Resistance then expanded. **Counterevidence and limits.** Fleming clearly discovered activity. Oxford’s team did not invent mold. Companies also took financial risks. Wartime urgency helped coordination. No single laboratory sufficed. Exact credit boundaries remain debatable.",
