      "id": "anesthesia-priority-conflict",
      "title": "Anesthesia had rival pioneers",
      "question": "Who made surgery painless?",
      "body": "**Documented fact.** Crawford Long used ether. His surgery occurred during 1842. He published much later. Horace Wells used nitrous oxide. His public demonstration failed. William Morton demonstrated ether publicly. That happened October 16, 1846. John Warren removed a tumor. Patient Edward Abbott reported little pain. Charles Jackson had advised Morton. Morton sought proprietary control. He called ether Letheon. The recipe soon emerged. Priority disputes then intensified. **Scholarly interpretation.** Long showed earlier use. Morton showed public reproducibility. Wells advanced dental anesthesia. Jackson supplied chemical advice. These roles differ. Dissemination depended upon demonstration. **Benefits.** Surgery became less agonizing. Longer operations became possible. **Conflicts and harms.** Rivalry consumed several careers. Proprietary claims impeded trust. Early anesthesia remained dangerous. Dosing lacked reliable instruments. **Counterevidence and limits.** Earlier pain-control practices existed. Ether use was not novel. Surgical anesthesia required acceptance. One demonstration cannot explain everything. Specific credit can be divided. Broader discovery remains collective.",
