      "question": "Who invented the solution?",
      "body": "**Documented fact.** Intestinal transport research came first. Glucose improved sodium absorption. Dhaka researchers tested therapy. David Nalin participated. Richard Cash participated too. Their 1968 trial involved cholera. Oral solution reduced intravenous needs. War displaced Bangladeshis during 1971. Refugee camps faced cholera. Intravenous supplies became scarce. Dilip Mahalanabis used oral therapy. Families helped administer it. Reported mortality stayed far lower. WHO later promoted standardized salts. UNICEF supported global distribution. **Scholarly interpretation.** Physiology enabled the formula. Clinical trials established efficacy. Field improvisation proved scalability. Public systems enabled reach. **Benefits.** Treatment became cheap. Caregivers could provide it. Millions avoided fatal dehydration. **Conflicts and harms.** Simplified stories crown individuals. Incorrect mixing can injure. Severe dehydration still needs intravenous care. Implementation required repeated community teaching. **Counterevidence and limits.** Camp evidence lacked randomization. Crisis comparisons were imperfect. Earlier work also mattered. No single inventor suffices. Collective credit better fits records.",
