      "id": "mrna-platform-network",
      "title": "mRNA vaccines combined platforms",
      "question": "Did one discovery suffice?",
      "body": "**Documented fact.** Scientists pursued messenger RNA for decades. Delivery remained difficult. Unmodified RNA triggered inflammation. Katalin Karikó persisted. Drew Weissman collaborated. Their modified nucleosides reduced responses. Protein production also improved. They published key results. Lipid carriers developed separately. Those particles protect fragile RNA. They support cellular entry. Companies then optimized formulations. Researchers selected coronavirus targets. Clinical trials tested vaccines. Regulators reviewed evidence. Manufacturers scaled production rapidly. The 2023 Nobel recognized Karikó. It also recognized Weissman. **Scholarly interpretation.** Their discovery removed one barrier. Lipid nanoparticles removed another. Genomics accelerated target design. Public investment supported readiness. **Benefits.** Vaccines arrived unusually quickly. Trials showed strong protection. Platforms enabled rapid redesign. **Conflicts and harms.** Access remained globally unequal. Cold chains constrained delivery. Rare adverse events required monitoring. **Counterevidence and limits.** Speed did not mean novelty. Decades of work preceded 2020. Nobel scope was specific. It did not rank every contributor. Platform success remains collective.",
