    {
      "id": "primitive-echo-negativity",
      "title": "Primitive Echo: danger sticks",
      "question": "Why keep scrolling threats?",
      "body": "**Documented evidence.** Negative information often weighs more. Adults show this pattern. Children show it too. Threat cues capture attention. Context changes the effect. Anxiety can strengthen it. Online systems measure engagement. Out-group hostility predicts sharing. Moral emotion also spreads. These findings differ importantly. Attention is not sharing. Sharing is not belief. **Evolutionary interpretation.** Missing danger could prove costly. Detecting false danger costs less. Selection may favor caution. Social warnings might protect groups. This remains an inference. Experiments cannot observe ancestral minds. **Current echo.** People keep scanning crises. Threatening posts feel urgent. Repetition prolongs attention. Platforms can amplify exposure. **Benefits.** Vigilance can prevent harm. Warnings coordinate fast responses. **Harms.** Continuous scanning increases distress. False alarms consume attention. Hostile content can polarize. **Counterevidence and limits.** Negativity is not universal. Positive information also spreads. Culture shapes media habits. Algorithms shape exposure strongly. Evolution cannot excuse design choices. Doomscrolling has multiple causes.",
